"CSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2537view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"lineno\":1600}); Total lines: 2555\nL1551:   * 【836†sibling-index-keyframe-value-dynamic.html†wpt.fyi】 【837†(live test)†wpt.live】 【838†(source)†github.com】\nL1552:   * 【839†sibling-index-linear-gradient-gcs.html†wpt.fyi】 【840†(live test)†wpt.live】 【841†(source)†github.com】\nL1553:   * 【842†tree-scoped-sibling-function.html†wpt.fyi】 【843†(live test)†wpt.live】 【844†(source)†github.com】\nL1554:   * 【845†trig-functions-with-runtime-angle-arguments.html†wpt.fyi】 【846†(live test)†wpt.live】 【847†(source)†github.com】\nL1555: The sibling-count() 【114†functional notation】 represents, as an 【721†<integer>】, the total number of child 【848†elements】 in the parent of the element on which the notation is used.\nL1556: \nL1557: The sibling-index() 【114†functional notation】 represents, as an 【721†<integer>】, the index of the element on which the notation is used among its 【849†inclusive siblings†dom.spec.whatwg.org】. Like 【850†:nth-child()】, 【725†sibling-index()】 is 1-indexed.\nL1558: \nL1559: Together, these are known as the tree-counting functions.\nL1560: Note: The 【851†counter()】 function can provide similar abilities as 【725†sibling-index()】, but returns a 【150†<string>】 rather than an 【721†<integer>】.\nL1561: \nL1562: When used on an 【852†element-backed pseudo-element】 that is also a real element, the 【853†tree-counting functions】 resolve for that real element. For other 【685†pseudo-elements】, they resolve as if they were resolved against the 【854†ultimate originating element】.\nL1563: A 【853†tree-counting function】 is a type of 【855†loosely-matched】 【856†tree-scoped reference】, which is resolved as if the element were given an automatic 【857†tree-scoped name】 that matches an identical automatic name on the tree-scoped reference of the tree-counting function. If the reference fails to match (because the element is, for example, in a descendent shadow tree from the stylesheet), the function returns 0.\nL1564: Note: This restriction is to avoid leaking shadow tree information to outer 【858†trees†dom.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1565: The following examples show how 【725†sibling-index()】 resolves for 【685†pseudo-elements】 and across 【859†shadow tree†dom.spec.whatwg.org】 boundaries:\nL1566: \nL1567:     #target {\nL1568:       /* Based on the sibling-index() of #target */\nL1569:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1570:     }\nL1571:     #target::before {\nL1572:       /* Based on the sibling-index() of #target */\nL1573:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1574:     }\nL1575:     #target::before::marker {\nL1576:       /* Based on the sibling-index() of #target */\nL1577:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1578:     }\nL1579:     ::slotted(*)::before {\nL1580:       /* Based on the sibling-index() of the slotted element in the outer tree */\nL1581:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1582:     }\nL1583:     ::part(my-part) {\nL1584:       /* Returns 0px, because referencing a shadow tree */\nL1585:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1586:     }\nL1587:     :host {\nL1588:       /* Based on the host's sibling-index() in the outer tree */\nL1589:       width: calc(sibling-index() * 10px);\nL1590:     }\nL1591: Note: Like 【850†:nth-child()】 and other counting selectors, the 【853†tree-counting functions】 operate on the DOM tree, rather than the 【860†flat tree】 like most CSS operations do. They may, in the future, have variants that support counting flat tree siblings.\nL1592: \nL1593: Note: These functions may, in the future, be extended to accept an of 【861†<complex-real-selector-list>】 argument, similar to 【850†:nth-child()】, to filter on a subset of the children.\nL1594: ## Appendix A: Arbitrary Substitution Functions\nL1595: \nL1596: An arbitrary substitution function is a 【114†functional notation】 that will, when resolved, substitute itself with other values that are unknowable at parse time.\nL1597: ### Substitution\nL1598: \nL1599: Each 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 must define how to replace an arbitrary substitution function for itself, given a list of arguments and a 【862†stack†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 of 【529†substitution contexts】, and likely involving further 【171†substitution】. It must return a sequence of 【168†component values】 (fully resolved, so no further arbitrary substitution functions exist in the sequence), which it will be replaced by.\nL1600: A function’s 【525†replacement】 algorithm is called with the results of 【78†early substitution】 and parsing with the function’s 【521†argument grammar】, but nothing more. The algorithm will have to further 【171†substitute】 each argument it deals with, before 【160†parsing】 it as the appropriate part of its normal grammar, and then performing whatever logic it needs.\nL1601: It might leave some arguments completely unsubstituted (for example, the fallback argument of an 【138†attr()】 function, if the attribute value exists and correctly parses), or only parse some arguments based on the results of evaluating other arguments (for example, later 【515†<if-condition>】 values in an 【115†if()】 function are only evaluated if earlier ones evaluated as false, and only the result in the \"successful\" 【514†<if-branch>】 is evaluated).\nL1602: To substitute arbitrary substitution functions in a sequence of 【168†component values】 `values`, given an optional 【529†substitution context】 `context`:\nL1603: \nL1604:   1. 【528†Guard】 `context` for the remainder of this algorithm. If `context` is marked as a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL1605:   2. 【526†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 `func` in `values` (ordered via a depth-first pre-order traversal) that is not nested in the contents of another arbitrary substitution function:\nL1606: \nL1607:     1. 【863†Substitute early-invoked functions】 in `func`’s contents, and let `early result` be the result.\nL1608: \nL1609:     2. If `early result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1610:     3. 【160†Parse】 `early result` according to `func`’s 【521†argument grammar】. If this returns failure, replace `func` in values with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】 and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】; otherwise, let `arguments` be the result.\nL1611: \nL1612:     4. 【525†Replace an arbitrary substitution function】 for `func`, given `arguments`, as defined by that function. Let `result` be the returned list of 【168†component values】.\nL1613:     5. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value. Otherwise, replace `func` in `values` with `result`.\nL1614: \nL1615:   3. If `context` is marked as a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】. Nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 may have marked `context` as 【530†cyclic】 in step 2.\nL1616: \nL1617:   4. Return `values`.\nL1618: A substitution context is a 【154†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 consisting of: the dependency type, as a string; one or more additional values, specific to the dependency type. (Usually, just one additional string.)\nL1619: \nL1620: As 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 are 【864†resolved†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】 they accumulate 【529†substitution contexts】 which affect how nested arbitrary substitution functions resolve.\nL1621: For example, while resolving a var(--foo) function, the value of the --foo property is fetched and 【171†substituted】, with a 【529†substitution context】 of `«\"property\", \"--foo\"»`, preventing any nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 from cyclicly depending on --foo as well.\nL1622: While resolving an attr(foo) function, the value of the foo attribute on the element is fetched and 【171†substituted】, with a 【529†substitution context】 of `«\"attribute\", \"foo\"»`, preventing any nested functions from cyclicly depending on the foo attribute as well.\nL1623: \nL1624: The types of 【529†substitution contexts】 are currently:\nL1625: \nL1626:   * \"property\", followed by a property name, and optionally a 【768†custom function】.\nL1627: \nL1628:   * \"attribute\", followed by an attribute name.\nL1629:   * \"function\", followed by a 【768†custom function】.\nL1630: \nL1631: As 【171†substitution】 is recursively invoked by nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 being 【525†replaced】, 【528†guards】 \"stack up\" the 【529†substitution contexts】 passed to each invocation.\nL1632: \nL1633: When a 【529†substitution context】 is guarded, it means that, for the duration of the guard, an attempt to guard a matching substitution context again will mark all substitution contexts involved in the cycle as cyclic substitution contexts.\nL1634: For example, given the following style:\nL1635: \nL1636:     .foo {\nL1637:       --one: var(--two);\nL1638:       --two: var(--one);\nL1639:     }\nL1640: 【531†Property replacement】 for --one invokes 【171†substitution】 with a 【529†substitution context】 of «\"property\", \"--one\"». Substitution sees the var(--two) function and invokes 【525†replace a var() function】, which fetches the values of --two and substitutes again, this time with a substitution context of «\"property\", \"--two\"».\nL1641: That substitution sees the var(--one) function and invokes replace a var() function, which fetches the value of --one and substitutes again, with a substitution context of «\"property\", \"--one\"».\nL1642: This, finally, is a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, since it matches the 【529†substitution context】 from the first 【171†substitution】, causing the substitution to just produce the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】. This percolates back up the nested invocations, eventually resulting in --one becoming 【486†invalid at computed-value time】. The same happens, in opposite order, when performing 【531†property replacement】 on --two.\nL1643: When a 【530†cycle is detected】, all participants in the cycle become invalid. For example, all of the following declarations become 【486†invalid at computed-value time】.\nL1644: \nL1645:     .foo {\nL1646:       --one: var(--two);\nL1647:       --two: var(--three, baz);\nL1648:       --three: var(--one);\nL1649:     }\nL1650: \nL1651: \nL1652: The presence of a fallback in `var(--three, baz)` does not affect the outcome.\nL1653: ### Argument Grammars and Spread Syntax\nL1654: \nL1655: Each 【126†arbitrary substitution function】, in addition to its standard grammar, must define an argument grammar: a much less specific and less restrictive version of its normal grammar, which serves solely to separate the function’s contents into distinct arguments.\nL1656: \nL1657: Note: Typically, an 【521†argument grammar】 will only consist of some punctuation (usually commas) and the 【117†<declaration-value>】 production. See the 【115†if()】 function for an example.\nL1658: Each function, in its 【525†replacement】 algorithm, will apply 【171†substitution】 to its arguments and then 【160†parse】 them according to the appropriate parts of its standard grammar. As it is in control of this process, however, some arguments can be left unresolved and unparsed.\nL1659: For example, the 【115†if()】 function’s 【521†argument grammar】 merely divides its value into alternating \"test\" and \"result\" arguments, separated by `:` and `;`. It then evaluates tests one by one, and only evaluates a single argument matching the first successful test.\nL1660: This allows 【115†if()】 to achieve behavior similar to `if` constructs in other programming languages, where later \"branches\" aren’t evaluated at all (beyond a basic parse) and thus can’t cause errors in cases that would be caught by earlier branches.\nL1661: This means the following is a valid declaration when the viewport is 600px or wider, resulting in the value 【865†blue】. Only when the viewport is narrower than 600px does the 【115†if()】 trigger cyclic behavior and cause --color to be 【486†invalid at computed-value time】.\nL1662: \nL1663:     .foo {\nL1664:       --color: if(media(width >= 600px): blue; else: var(--color));\nL1665:     }\nL1666: This also means that, ordinarily, parsing according to a function’s 【521†argument grammar】 does not see the results of any nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】; the contents are divided into arguments based only on the values literally present inside the function.\nL1667: For example, in random-item(auto, var(--foo), var(--bar)), the 【122†random-item()】 function selects between two random values, either the result of var(--foo) or var(--bar). This is true even if one of them contains commas, like:\nL1668: \nL1669:     .random-fonts {\nL1670:       --foo: Courier, monospace;\nL1671:       --bar: Arial, serif;\nL1672:       font-family: random-item(auto, var(--foo), var(--bar));\nL1673:       /* equivalent to: */\nL1674:       font-family: random-item(auto, {Courier, monospace}, {Arial, serif});\nL1675:       /* and thus, randomly, equivalent to either */\nL1676:       font-family: Courier, monospace;\nL1677:       /* or */\nL1678:       font-family: Arial, serif;\nL1679:     }\nL1680: This behavior ensures that authors don’t have to defensively wrap any arguments containing 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 in {} characters; what you see is what you get.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2537view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"lineno\":1810}); Total lines: 2555\nL1766: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as 【919†color】, it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL1767: \nL1768: While a 【127†var()】 function can’t get a 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 from the 【542†custom property】 itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger 【920†explicit defaulting】 for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL1769: \nL1770:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL1771: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, the 【127†var()】 will instead substitute in the 【921†initial】 keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally 【919†color: initial】. This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL1772: \nL1773: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL1774:   1. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the 【529†substitution context】. Let `result` be the returned 【168†component value】 sequence.\nL1775: \nL1776:   2. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1777: \nL1778:   3. 【160†Parse】 `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1779:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL1780: \nL1781: Tests\nL1782:   * 【922†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【923†(live test)†wpt.live】 【924†(source)†github.com】\nL1783:   * 【925†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【926†(live test)†wpt.live】 【927†(source)†github.com】\nL1784:   * 【928†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【929†(live test)†wpt.live】 【930†(source)†github.com】\nL1785:   * 【931†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【932†(live test)†wpt.live】 【933†(source)†github.com】\nL1786:   * 【934†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【935†(live test)†wpt.live】 【936†(source)†github.com】\nL1787:   * 【937†variable-declaration-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【938†(live test)†wpt.live】 【939†(source)†github.com】\nL1788:   * 【940†variable-declaration-05.html†wpt.fyi】 【941†(live test)†wpt.live】 【942†(source)†github.com】\nL1789:   * 【943†variable-generated-content-dynamic-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【944†(live test)†wpt.live】 【945†(source)†github.com】\nL1790:   * 【946†variable-presentation-attribute.html†wpt.fyi】 【947†(live test)†wpt.live】 【948†(source)†github.com】\nL1791:   * 【949†variable-reference-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【950†(live test)†wpt.live】 【951†(source)†github.com】\nL1792:   * 【952†variable-reference-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【953†(live test)†wpt.live】 【954†(source)†github.com】\nL1793:   * 【955†variable-reference-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【956†(live test)†wpt.live】 【957†(source)†github.com】\nL1794:   * 【958†variable-reference-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【959†(live test)†wpt.live】 【960†(source)†github.com】\nL1795:   * 【961†variable-reference-05.html†wpt.fyi】 【962†(live test)†wpt.live】 【963†(source)†github.com】\nL1796:   * 【964†variable-reference-12.html†wpt.fyi】 【965†(live test)†wpt.live】 【966†(source)†github.com】\nL1797:   * 【967†variable-reference-16.html†wpt.fyi】 【968†(live test)†wpt.live】 【969†(source)†github.com】\nL1798:   * 【970†variable-reference-40.html†wpt.fyi】 【971†(live test)†wpt.live】 【972†(source)†github.com】\nL1799:   * 【973†variable-reference-refresh.html†wpt.fyi】 【974†(live test)†wpt.live】 【975†(source)†github.com】\nL1800:   * 【976†variable-substitution-background-properties.html†wpt.fyi】 【977†(live test)†wpt.live】 【978†(source)†github.com】\nL1801:   * 【979†variable-substitution-basic.html†wpt.fyi】 【980†(live test)†wpt.live】 【981†(source)†github.com】\nL1802:   * 【982†variable-substitution-filters.html†wpt.fyi】 【983†(live test)†wpt.live】 【984†(source)†github.com】\nL1803:   * 【985†variable-substitution-replaced-size.html†wpt.fyi】 【986†(live test)†wpt.live】 【987†(source)†github.com】\nL1804:   * 【988†variable-substitution-shadow-properties.html†wpt.fyi】 【989†(live test)†wpt.live】 【990†(source)†github.com】\nL1805:   * 【991†variable-substitution-variable-declaration.html†wpt.fyi】 【992†(live test)†wpt.live】 【993†(source)†github.com】\nL1806: CSSOM\nL1807: \nL1808:   * 【994†variable-reference-cssom.html†wpt.fyi】 【995†(live test)†wpt.live】 【996†(source)†github.com】\nL1809: \nL1810: * * *\nL1811: \nL1812: Note that 【171†substitution】 takes place at the level of CSS tokens 【997†[css-syntax-3]】, not at a textual level; you can’t build up a single token where part of it is provided by a variable:\nL1813: \nL1814:     .foo {\nL1815:       --gap: 20;\nL1816:       margin-top: var(--gap)px;\nL1817:     }\nL1818: This is not equivalent to setting 【776†margin-top: 20px;】 (a length). Instead, it’s equivalent to margin-top: 20 px; (a number followed by an ident), which is simply an invalid value for the margin-top property. Note, though, that 【420†calc()】 can be used to validly achieve the same thing, like so:\nL1819: \nL1820:     .foo {\nL1821:       --gap: 20;\nL1822:       margin-top: calc(var(--gap) * 1px);\nL1823:     }\nL1824: This also implies that the post-substitution value might not be directly serializable as-is. Here’s a similar example to the preceding:\nL1825: \nL1826:     .foo {\nL1827:       --gap: 20;\nL1828:       --not-px-length: var(--gap)px;\nL1829:     }\nL1830: The serialization of the computed (post-substitution) value of --not-px-length is not 20px, because that would parse back as the single combined dimension; instead, it will serialize with a comment between the two tokens, like px, to enforce that they are separate tokens even when re-parsing.\nL1831: \nL1832: Tests\nL1833:   * 【998†variable-declaration-14.html†wpt.fyi】 【999†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1000†(source)†github.com】\nL1834:   * 【1001†variable-declaration-53.html†wpt.fyi】 【1002†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1003†(source)†github.com】\nL1835:   * 【1004†variable-declaration-54.html†wpt.fyi】 【1005†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1006†(source)†github.com】\nL1836:   * 【1007†variable-declaration-55.html†wpt.fyi】 【1008†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1009†(source)†github.com】\nL1837:   * 【1010†variable-reference-15.html†wpt.fyi】 【1011†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1012†(source)†github.com】\nL1838:   * 【1013†variable-reference-without-whitespace.html†wpt.fyi】 【1014†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1015†(source)†github.com】\nL1839: ### Invalid Substitution\nL1840: \nL1841: When 【531†property replacement】 results in a property’s value containing the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, this makes the declaration invalid at computed-value time. When this happens, the computed value is one of the following depending on the property’s type:\nL1842: \nL1843: The property is a non-registered 【542†custom property】\nL1844: The property is a 【139†registered custom property†drafts.css-houdini.org】 with 【163†universal syntax†drafts.css-houdini.org】\nL1845: The computed value is the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL1846: \nL1847: Otherwise\nL1848: \nL1849: \nL1850: Either the property’s inherited value or its initial value depending on whether the property is inherited or not, respectively, as if the property’s value had been specified as the 【885†unset】 keyword.\nL1851: \nL1852: Tests\nL1853: \nL1854:   * 【1016†variables-substitute-guaranteed-invalid.html†wpt.fyi】 【1017†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1018†(source)†github.com】\nL1855: For example, in the following code:\nL1856: \nL1857:     :root { --not-a-color: 20px; }\nL1858:     p { background-color: red; }\nL1859:     p { background-color: var(--not-a-color); }\nL1860: \nL1861: \nL1862: the <p> elements will have transparent backgrounds (the initial value for 【916†background-color】), rather than red backgrounds. The same would happen if the 【542†custom property】 itself was unset, or contained an invalid 【127†var()】 function.\nL1863: Note the difference between this and what happens if the author had just written 【916†background-color: 20px】 directly in their stylesheet - that would be a normal syntax error, which would cause the rule to be discarded, so the background-color: red rule would be used instead.\nL1864: Note: The 【486†invalid at computed-value time】 concept exists because 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 can’t \"fail early\" like other syntax errors can, so by the time the user agent realizes a property value is invalid, it’s already thrown away the other cascaded values.\nL1865: ### Substitution in Shorthand Properties\nL1866: \nL1867: 【126†Arbitrary substitution functions】 produce some complications when parsing 【541†shorthand properties】 into their component longhands, and when serializing shorthand properties from their component longhands.\nL1868: If a 【541†shorthand property】 contains an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 in its value, the 【1019†longhand properties】 it’s associated with must instead be filled in with a special, unobservable-to-authors pending-substitution value that indicates the shorthand contains an arbitrary substitution function, and thus the longhand’s value can’t be determined until after 【171†substituted】.\nL1869: This value must then be cascaded as normal, and at computed-value time, after 【171†substitution】, the shorthand must be parsed and the longhands must be given their appropriate values at that point.\nL1870: \nL1871: Tests\nL1872:   * 【1020†variable-reference-36.html†wpt.fyi】 【1021†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1022†(source)†github.com】\nL1873:   * 【1023†variable-reference-37.html†wpt.fyi】 【1024†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1025†(source)†github.com】\nL1874:   * 【1026†variable-reference-38.html†wpt.fyi】 【1027†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1028†(source)†github.com】\nL1875:   * 【1029†variable-substitution-shorthands.html†wpt.fyi】 【1030†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1031†(source)†github.com】\nL1876:   * 【1032†vars-background-shorthand-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【1033†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1034†(source)†github.com】\nL1877:   * 【1035†vars-font-shorthand-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【1036†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1037†(source)†github.com】\nL1878: Note: When a shorthand is written without an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】, it is parsed and separated out into its component 【1019†longhand properties】 at parse time; the longhands then participate in the 【482†cascade】, with the 【541†shorthand property】 more or less discarded. When the shorthand contains a 【127†var()】, however, this can’t be done, as the var() could be substituted with anything.\nL1879: 【1038†Pending-substitution values】 must be serialized as the empty string, if an API allows them to be observed.\nL1880: \nL1881: Tests\nL1882: \nL1883:   * 【1039†variable-definition-border-shorthand-serialize.html†wpt.fyi】 【1040†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1041†(source)†github.com】\nL1884:   * 【1042†vars-border-shorthand-serialize.html†wpt.fyi】 【1043†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1044†(source)†github.com】\nL1885: \nL1886: * * *\nL1887: 【541†Shorthand properties】 are serialized by gathering the values of their component 【1019†longhand properties】, and synthesizing a value that will parse into the same set of values.\nL1888: \nL1889: If all of the component 【1019†longhand properties】 for a given 【541†shorthand】 are 【1038†pending-substitution values】 from the same original shorthand value, the shorthand property must serialize to that original (【126†arbitrary substitution function】-containing) value.\nL1890: Otherwise, if any of the component 【1019†longhand properties】 for a given 【541†shorthand】 are 【1038†pending-substitution values】, or contain 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 of their own that have not yet been 【171†substituted】, the shorthand property must serialize to the empty string.\nL1891: ### Safely Handling Overly-Long Substitution\nL1892: \nL1893: Naively implemented, some 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 (such as 【127†var()】) can be used in a variation of the \"billion laughs attack\":\nL1894: \nL1895:     .foo {\nL1896:       --prop1: lol;\nL1897:       --prop2: var(--prop1) var(--prop1);\nL1898:       --prop3: var(--prop2) var(--prop2);\nL1899:       --prop4: var(--prop3) var(--prop3);\nL1900:       /* etc */\nL1901:     }\nL1902: In this short example, --prop4’s computed value is lol lol lol lol lol lol lol lol, containing 8 copies of the original lol. Every additional level added to this doubles the number of identifiers; extending it to a mere 30 levels, the work of a few minutes by hand, would make --prop30 contain nearly a billion instances of the identifier.\nL1903: To avoid this sort of attack, UAs must impose a UA-defined limit on the allowed length of the token stream that an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 expands into. If an arbitrary substitution function would expand into a longer token stream than this limit, it instead is replaced with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL1904: \nL1905: Tests\nL1906:   * 【1045†long-variable-reference-crash.html†wpt.fyi】 【1046†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1047†(source)†github.com】\nL1907:   * 【1048†variable-exponential-blowup.html†wpt.fyi】 【1049†(live test)†wpt.live】 【1050†(source)†github.com】\nL1908: \nL1909: This specification does not define what size limit should be imposed. However, since there are valid use-cases for custom properties that contain a kilobyte or more of text, it’s recommended that the limit be set relatively high.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2537view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"id\":529}); Total lines: 2555\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2537view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"id\":80}); Total lines: 2555\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn2537view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view0\",\"id\":138}); Total lines: 1339\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2537view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2523view1\",\"id\":65}); Total lines: 2555\n"
